edb/ref 17592186442945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442956 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442956 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186442949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442956 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442956 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442956 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186442947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442956 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c1c519c6-ee9a-53ca-a030-5c374bc6f933" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442957 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442957 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35629" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442957 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442957 #edb/ref 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:* \n\n*$\\alpha 0=0$ for any $\\alpha \\in R$*\n\n*Proof:* \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0 & =\\alpha (0+0) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0+\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0) & =(\\alpha 0+\\alpha 0)+(-\\alpha 0) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-\\alpha 0\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0) & =\\alpha 0+(\\alpha 0+(-\\alpha 0)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =\\alpha 0+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0 & =\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442957 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2473766c-4484-58b4-94f0-d8e70773f841" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442957 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0 & =\\alpha (0+0) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0+\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A8}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0-\\alpha 0 & =(\\alpha 0+\\alpha 0)-\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{add }-\\alpha 0\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ \\alpha 0-\\alpha 0 & =\\alpha 0+(\\alpha 0-\\alpha 0) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =\\alpha 0+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A4}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =\\alpha 0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442958 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442958 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442958 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442958 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $P$ be the set of all ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that $xy=0.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$P$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to the usual component-wise addition and scalar multiplication in ${R}^{2}$) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442958 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d3e3740-f421-5f05-912d-ea42f86d4dd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442958 #edb/ref 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$P$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to the usual component-wise addition and scalar multiplication in ${R}^{2}$) because **addition of ordered pairs in $P$ is not closed.** *  \n\nFor example, $(1,0)\\in P$ because $1\\cdot 0=0$ and $(0,1)\\in P$ because $0\\cdot 1=0.$ However, if we add the two pairs component-wise, we get\n\n$$\n(1,0)+(0,1)=(1+0,0+1)=(1,1).\n$$\n\nThe product of the components of this pair is $1\\cdot 1=1\\ne 0.$ So, $(1,0)+(0,1)\\notin P.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Multiplication of $(x,y)\\in P$ by a scalar is closed. Indeed, if $\\alpha \\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\alpha \\cdot (x,y)=(\\alpha x,\\alpha y)\\in P\n$$\n\nsince $(\\alpha x)\\cdot (\\alpha y)={\\alpha }^{2}{{(xy)}_{⏟}}_{=0}=0.$\n$\\,✓$\n\n- The zero element in $P$ is $(0,0).$ $✓$\n\n- For an ordered pair $(x,y)\\in P,$ the ordered pair $-(x,y)=(-x,-y)$ is also an element of $P$ since $(-x)\\cdot (-y)=xy=0.$\n$\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot (x,y)=(x,y)$ for all $(x,y)\\in P.$ $\\,✓$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442959 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186442956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442959 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43858" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442959 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442959 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V$ be the set of all  $2\\times 2$ invertible matrices with real entries, with the usual matrix addition and scalar multiplication. Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$V$ is **not** a vector space  because $\\text{____}\\,.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442959 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "836e7d2e-0ac3-5b0c-b2c3-d01db384e22c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186442959 #edb/ref 1074 "The correct answer is \n\n*$V$ is **not** a vector space  because **the $2\\times 2$ zero matrix is not in $V.$** *\n\nIndeed, the zero matrix doesn't have an inverse, so it doesn't belong to $V.$ As a result, $V$ cannot be a vector space.\n\nOn the other hand, \n\n- Addition of matrices is associative.  $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of matrices is commutative.  $\\,✓$\n\n- The $2\\times 2$ identity matrix is invertible, so it belongs to $V.$  $\\,✓$\n\n- If $A$ is invertible then $-A$ is invertible too. So, $-A\\in V.$ $\\,✓$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
